{
  "case_id": "2009-08-724",
  "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:06 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "GEN. RICHARD CORDRAY",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CORDRAY: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Because this case arises under the deferential standards of the AEDPA statute, Mr. Spisak must show that the Ohio Supreme Court's decision was contrary to Mills v. Maryland or that it unreasonably applied Strickland v. Washington."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "GEN. RICHARD CORDRAY",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0000-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CORDRAY: -- on that question, the consequence would be that the court would then impose some version of a life sentence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "GEN. RICHARD CORDRAY",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CORDRAY: This, as Your Honor notes from your time on the trial court, is the jury dynamic in the jury room. It is the push towards unanimity. The issue here is whether the Constitution requires an instruction to be given that would encourage a single juror to hold out and try to avoid reaching a unanimous verdict. The Court has never held that that is constitutionally required, and if they did so hold in this case it would be an extension that is a new rule and would not be applicable on AEDPA review here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Have you ever heard or read a defense summation that was more derogatory of the defendant than the summation here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michael J. Benza",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, counsel presented and argued to the jury nonstatutory aggravating factors as reasons to impose the death sentence on Mr. Spisak."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michael J. Benza",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Sixth Circuit evaluated performance of trial counsel in this case and found deficient performance for three primary areas."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Benza, do you know of any case where ineffective assistance was found on the basis of a closing argument alone?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Well, why wouldn't you do\nthat as well, then, even a fortiori, where I'm just an\nappeals court judge and I get a district court opinion?\nMost common thing in the world, summary judgment denied,\nmotion denied, this denied, denied, denied."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Benza",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BENZA: I think AEDPA was designed that\nwhen the State courts, in fact, are doing their jobs\nunder the Constitution to protect defendants' rights to\nreview their claim, then they should receive the\nprotections of AEDPA."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Does Teague say anything\nabout time?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ohio Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CORDRAY: I think that the Ohio Supreme Court is entitled to deference because it adjudicated a claim on the merits, so -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ohio Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0003-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As to prejudice, the Ohio Supreme Court at 309(a) through 311(a) itself independently reweighed the aggravating factors against the mitigating factors, which is something the Ohio Supreme Court does under Ohio law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-724",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "05bce977a6d6ffb67aec8b8047d4a15f1cfa335b8b403f051322fd48f65352a2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-05bce977a6d6-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
